# Drafted by Gemini (R11 batch b4), edited. weston_murdoch's first conversation.

~ start
set weston_murdoch_met
Narrator: Sawdust and damp mortar scent the half-roofed chapel. A sandy-haired young man lowers his mallet, his smile bright enough to warm the cold stones.
weston_murdoch [smile]: Come in, friends, and rest your travel-sore feet! The roof still leaks a bit, but the Brightsingers turn no soul away from the song.
-> menu

~ menu
weston_murdoch [smile]: Tell me, brothers and sisters, what weighs upon you tonight?
* Ask for a Brightsinger blessing for the road -> service_open
* Ask about his work rebuilding the chapel -> work
* Ask how the village takes to his preaching -> village
* [Insight DC 14] Notice his flinch when the Oak of Many Faces is named -> secret_ok | secret_fail
* Take our leave -> END

~ service_open
weston_murdoch [smile]: Bow your heads and let the Dawn-Bird's grace mend what the dark has bruised. So long as breath remains, no soul is beyond a second verse.
-> menu

~ work
weston_murdoch [smile]: My Joan and our little ones, Peter and Charlotte, haul shingles right alongside me. Every nail we drive is a promise that mercy has a home here.
-> menu

~ village
weston_murdoch [weary]: Mayor Somerton prefers his old roots and blood-custom, and he looks sideways at our hymns. Still, fearful hearts grow tired of harsh gods.
-> menu

~ secret_ok
Narrator: His fingers tighten on the hammer shaft, his knuckles whitening as he looks toward the square where the great carved oak stands.
weston_murdoch [sad]: I preach redemption because I must believe in it, friend. My own mother and father died on the headsman's block, and I will not let their sin be my children's heritage.
-> menu

~ secret_fail
weston_murdoch [smile]: Only a chill draft through the rafters, friend. A man cannot look at that grim tree in the square without wishing for warmer light.
-> menu
